      "latency_ms": 11743.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 1130,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, forming the molecule **H₂O**. It is one of the most essential and abundant substances on Earth, critical to life and numerous natural processes. Here's a breakdown of its key characteristics:\n\n### **Chemical Composition**\n- **Molecular Structure**: Water is a polar molecule, with oxygen (more electronegative) pulling electrons away from hydrogen, creating a slight negative charge on the oxygen and positive charges on the hydrogens. This polarity enables hydrogen bonding between water molecules, which underpins many of its unique properties.\n\n### **Physical Properties**\n- **States of Matter**: Water exists in three states:\n  - **Liquid**: The most common form at room temperature.\n  - **Solid**: Ice (frozen water), which is less dense than liquid water, allowing it to float.\n  - **Gas**: Water vapor, formed through evaporation or boiling.\n- **Unique Properties**:\n  - **High Specific Heat**: Absorbs and releases heat slowly, stabilizing temperatures in organisms and environments.\n  - **Cohesion & Adhesion**: Molecules stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion), enabling capillary action in plants and surface tension.\n  - **Solvent Ability**: Dissolves many substances, earning it the title \"universal solvent,\" which is vital for biochemical reactions.\n\n### **Biological Importance**\n- **Life-Sustaining**: All known forms of life require water. It:\n  - Acts as a medium for metabolic reactions.\n  - Transports nutrients and waste in organisms.\n  - Regulates body temperature through sweating and respiration.\n  - Composes about 60% of the human body, supporting cellular functions.\n\n### **Environmental Role**\n- **Hydrological Cycle**: Water cycles through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff, distributing it across Earth's ecosystems.\n- **Distribution**: Most of Earth's water is saline (oceans), with only ~2.5% being fresh, and a small fraction accessible for human use (e.g., rivers, lakes, groundwater).\n\n### **Common Uses**\n- **Drinking and Hydration**: Essential for human and animal survival.\n- **Agriculture and Industry**: Used in irrigation, manufacturing, and energy production (e.g., hydroelectric power).\n- **Cleaning and Cooling**: Widely utilized in daily activities and industrial processes.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple yet extraordinary molecule whose unique properties make it indispensable for life, environmental stability, and human activities. Its ability to exist in multiple states and its role as a universal solvent highlight its fundamental importance in both natural and biological systems."
    },
